    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the membership-level-4 tenure rule in `Owner.getMembershipLevel()` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`).\n\n**What I found:** The existing `getMembershipLevel()` built a level from two pre-tenure factors \u2014 +1 for having an email, +1 for being unique (`namesakeCount == 0`) \u2014 starting at 1 and hard-capped at 3, with a comment noting \"level 4 is reserved for tenure.\" But level 4 was never actually awarded.\n\n**The change:**\n- Kept the pre-tenure factors capped at 3 (`Math.min(level, 3)`).\n- Added a tenure factor: when `registrationDate` is set and tenure (days from `registrationDate` to today, via `ChronoUnit.DAYS.between`) exceeds 365, the level becomes 4.\n- Added the `java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit` import and updated the Javadoc to describe the 1\u20134 range and the tenure rule.\n\n**Why this satisfies the spec:** A newly created owner has a `registrationDate` of today, so its tenure is 0 days \u2014 never more than 365 \u2014 so a new owner can never reach level 4. The acceptance test's new owner (email present, `namesakeCount` 0, 3-member household) maxes out the pre-tenure factors at 3 and stays there. The household size is not a factor in the level calculation (it never was), matching the spec's example of level 3.\n\n**Tests:** The full suite (the one `AcceptanceTest` class, including the new `coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3` checkpoint) passes \u2014 `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, BUILD SUCCESS.",
